Requirement: In order for the program to function, you must name the file autounattend.xml and copy it to the root of the Windows 11 installer stick/media you made with Rufus/Ventoy/Media Creation Tool. If the file is not in the correct location (the folder you see when you double-click to open the Win11 stick) and not named correctly (autounattend.xml), the Win11 installer will ignore the file and ask you all the setup questions manually.

What it actually does:

Skips the hardware checks (TPM, Secure Boot, RAM) so it will install on machines that don't officially qualify for Win11
Sets up keyboard/language for three locales at once (English, Romanian, and Norwegian layouts)
Skips the Microsoft account push — sets up a local account instead, no internet required during setup
Strips out the usual nags: Bing search integration, widgets, app suggestions, Edge's first-run popups, and "consumer feature" ads baked into the Start menu
Applies these same settings to any new account you create later, not just the first one — so you don't need to redo this per user
Cleans up after itself: deletes leftover setup files (including anything that might have your WiFi password saved in plaintext) once first login is complete

Everything runs automatically from a set of scripts bundled inside the single XML file — no extra USB partitions or separate files needed; it unpacks itself during setup.

Caveats:

Requires a valid Windows 11 license — this only automates setup, it doesn't touch activation
The hardware bypass flags are meant for lab/testing use, not something I'd push to a work fleet
No drivers included — that's still manual

The online and offline versions are meant to install Windows 11 Pro with language support for the following locales: English (US), Romanian, and Norwegian.

Offline vs. Online warning: Please make sure NOT to choose offline unless you actually plan to install Windows 11 without a WiFi/LAN/Ethernet connection. This can reduce installation time, as tested, to 15–20 minutes, but it will require you to plug in a USB cable or use Android USB tethering afterward, since you'll need to install drivers later.

Warning: Once you've selected the SSD, be careful — the installation will begin as soon as you press Next.

After installation, the script makes OOBE ask you for a network connection (choose "I don't have network" for the offline version, or connect to WiFi/LAN if using the online version), and to set up a username and password for the local account (password is not mandatory — you can click Next if you don't need one; the advantage is that you won't need a password to unlock/start your Windows 11 laptop anymore).

After this, Windows 11 will begin the update process, which is slow — I can't fix Microsoft's servers, unfortunately :))))

That said, at some point the online version will restart 3 times (since Windows updates and installs newer drivers) and then let you into the Windows 11 desktop with its normal UI.

Again, the offline version skips the update process and makes writing Windows 11 to disk faster, but you'll still need to wait a while for Windows updates afterward, since they're needed to install drivers (and this makes the overall process more automatic, at least from my point of view, in this context).



